      <h1>Global Storage Battery Market: The 2025 Record</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Market Intelligence · Last reviewed 2026-09-17</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> The world installed roughly <strong>315 GWh of battery storage in
        2025</strong> — about <strong>50% growth</strong> on 2024 (Benchmark Mineral Intelligence) — with
        <strong>China accounting for ~60%</strong> of additions (IEA), the United States and Europe following,
        and deployment widening into Australia, the Middle East and beyond. It is the fastest layer of the
        battery industry, and it now runs on its own engine rather than the EV market's.
      </div>

      <h2>Market snapshot</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Geography</th><td>Global</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Year</th><td>2025 (2024 comparison)</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Battery scope</th><td>Grid-scale + commercial + residential storage (all chemistries)</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Unit</th><td>GWh energy / GW power — both, stated per figure</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Data source</th><td>Benchmark Mineral Intelligence, IEA Global Energy Review (see Sources)</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Method</th><td>Primary statistics first (customs, MIIT, IEA, LME where available); industry research estimates otherwise; conflicting figures are flagged and explained, never averaged — see <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Confidence</th><td>Medium — Benchmark Mineral Intelligence and IEA estimates</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The headline numbers</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>2024</th><th>2025</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Installations (energy)</td><td>~210 GWh (implied)</td><td>~315 GWh</td><td><strong>~+50%</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Installations (power)</td><td>~73 GW</td><td>~106 GW</td><td><strong>~+43%</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>China share of additions</td><td>—</td><td>~60%</td><td>Leader by a wide margin</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Two sources, two metrics — the GWh figure counts energy capacity, the GW figure counts power; they move
        together because duration changes slowly (see the calculation below).
      </p>

      <h2>Our calculation</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>The average duration, derived:</strong> 315 GWh ÷ 106 GW ≈ <strong>3.0 hours</strong> — the
        average storage system installed in 2025 carries about three hours of energy at rated power, up from the
        1–2 hour systems that dominated earlier years as longer-duration use cases entered the mix.</p>
        <p><strong>The share of the lithium market:</strong> storage at ~315 GWh against roughly 1,500 GWh of
        total lithium cell output in 2025 means storage now absorbs about <strong>a fifth of lithium
        production</strong> — a structural shift from the days when EV traction took effectively all of it.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>What the growth rests on</h2>
      <p>
        Three engines turn at once: the <strong>cost curve</strong> (cells near
        <a href="/battery-price-trends/">$108/kWh</a> make storage bankable without subsidy in more markets each
        year), <strong>grid needs</strong> (renewables' variability creates the arbitrage and capacity markets
        storage serves), and <strong>policy</strong> (China's mandates, the US IRA, EU flexibility markets). The
        combination explains why storage grew through a year when EV demand wobbled — the two segments are
        finally running on different cycles.
      </p>

      <h2>The two-speed structure</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Character</th><th>Growth engine</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Utility-scale</td><td>GWh-class projects, long-duration creeping up</td><td>Grid arbitrage + capacity markets</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Commercial &amp; industrial</td><td>Peak shaving, backup</td><td>Electricity price differentials</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Residential</td><td>Solar-paired, kWh-class</td><td>Retail tariffs + resilience</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        The same two-speed shape as the wider industry — utility-scale carries the volume, residential carries
        the breadth, and the commercial layer sits between them on cost.
      </p>

      <h2>The conflicting estimates, explained</h2>
      <p>
        Storage market sizes conflict because the <strong>boundary is genuinely fuzzy</strong>: some counts
        include only grid-connected projects, others add residential and C&amp;I; some report power (GW), others
        energy (GWh); and the conversion between them depends on assumed duration. The honest reading: treat the
        ~300 GWh / ~100 GW / ~50% growth figures as the reliable layer — the direction and the rate — rather
        than any single vendor's dollar total (see <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My read:</strong> Storage just became the battery industry's second engine — a fifth of
        lithium demand, growing at 50% while EVs grow at 20% — and the structural consequence is that battery
        economics can no longer be read through the car window alone.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> Two engines mean two cycles: an EV slowdown no longer means a battery slowdown,
        and a storage boom no longer needs an EV boom to carry it. For buyers, suppliers and forecasters, the
        practical shift is the same — storage's demand, cost and pricing must be modelled as its own market
        now, because it has earned one.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not investment or purchasing advice.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the quoted market figure in GW (power) or GWh (energy), and what duration does it assume?</li>
        <li>Does the number include residential and commercial, or only grid-connected projects?</li>
        <li>What is the project's assumed cycling duty — daily, or a different schedule — for the warranty claim?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Benchmark Mineral Intelligence — ~315 GWh of battery storage installed worldwide in 2025, ~50% year-on-year growth.</li>
        <li>IEA Global Energy Review 2026 — China led 2025 deployment at ~60% of global additions; deployment widening beyond the largest markets.</li>
        <li>Industry reporting — 106 GW of new capacity in 2025 (+43% on 73 GW in 2024), GW-metric series.</li>
      </ul>

